 orders of its vertices in which every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge goes from an earlier vertex to a later one, in lexicographic order of the vertices’","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"positions in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". A graph with a cycle (a self-loop is one) has none, and the empty","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"graph has one, the empty order.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"AllTopologicalSorts"}]},"doc://Traversal/documentation/Traversal/DepthFirstSearch":{"title":"DepthFirstSearch","url":"\/documentation\/traversal\/depthfirstsearch","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DepthFirstSearch","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/DepthFirstSearch","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A depth-first search: a lazy sequence of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DepthFirstSearchEvent"},{"type":"text","text":"s, with every edge classified as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"a tree, back, forward or cross edge. It holds a copy of the graph and can be iterated more than"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"once. The search keeps its own stack, so a deep graph cannot overflow the call stack."}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"DepthFirstSearch","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Traversal/documentation/Traversal/depthFirstSearch(from:depthLimit:successors:)":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"depthFirstSearch"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Successors"},{"kind":"text","text":">("},{"kind":"externalParam","text":"from"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"some"},{"kind":"text","t